rota lap 4214, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; its test is to take the concrete fact in at the door before arguing with it, and the concrete fact at my door was a stash entry rather than a plan
think The round open stashed a working tree and left stash@{0} holding seven files stamped 210725 -- mantra/src/weave.rye, its merge control, a REDS row, a session log, a folded shelf, the card and the day index. That is %499's fourth firing on this seat, and the earth row's own instruction is to trust the first reading: a whole proven lap already existed, so writing a new one would have been the plausible story over the concrete fact.
think I recovered by reading the TREE rather than the blob -- git checkout stash@{0} -- <paths> -- because a blob carries no mode and the exec-bit law names that exact loss. Three files came back at their recorded modes.
think I did not recover REDS.md or ITINERARY.md from the stash, and that mattered. The stash was cut against an older HEAD whose ledger headline read 587 rows; HEAD reads 588, so checking those two files out whole would have reverted a peer's row and a peer's card block in one move.
think So the row was re-seated by hand onto the current pin instead, and its number re-derived: git fetch xy, then reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next, which answered 589. The parked lap had booked 589 from a spine one row shorter, and the two agreed by luck rather than by method. The stamp 20260907.210632 is what actually identifies the row.
think Re-proving came before trusting. Both rostered witnesses and the control ran here on a settled tree rather than being taken from the parked log's word.
obs mantra_weave_merge GREEN -- nine claims, eight breaks caught. mantra_weave_annotate GREEN -- five readings over thirteen phases, ten breaks caught. mantra_weave_merge_control control_verdict=ok across ten phases. The parked work stands on this tree, not only on the one it was written against.
obs The report card reads mantra/src/weave.rye at 88, B+, Field -- register 83, reach 70, truth 100 over zero unresolved cited paths, service 100 on 54 living citers with the card naming the module. B or better stands, so no molt frame.
obs My first hot roster pass died at exit 144 after 53 lines with every guard green -- signal 16, which is the shape %587 and %569 both name for a fleet pattern-kill. No peer pass was running in my tree; the only --scoped pass on the pier had its cwd in grain-grass. I cannot attribute it, and that is the row's own finding arriving from the other side.
obs What %587 did not name is that the VICTIM has a receipt even though the killer leaves none. Exit 144 and a truncated output file are both concrete, and they are what let this lap say a pass was killed rather than that it failed. A guard reading a runner's exit for signal 16 could count what the process table forgets.
obs Adding the row put construction/REDS.md at 41,519 bytes against its 40,960 bound, so equinox_e123_living_pin_guard read pin_over_bound. Folded %589 to its own shelf through reds_fold.sh -- rows_moved=1, recital written -- and the pin reads 38,691. The ledger spine is GREEN at 589 rows with no gaps.
obs The card block cost more bytes than the elder it replaced, and the card sits at its ceiling, so the block was cut four times to fit: 41,557 then 41,353, 41,231, 41,079, and 40,944 against 40,960. Every cut was prose rather than a claim -- the falsifier quote, one repetition, and two hedges.
obs And the number collided anyway. While I was proving and folding, a peer published %589 bound to stamp 20260907.210124 -- five minutes earlier than mine. The derived spine's rule decides it without a judgment call: the earlier stamp keeps the number, and my unshared row renumbers to %590 while keeping stamp 20260907.210632. Four files moved, all of them mine and none of them published.
obs That is exactly the shape %230, %252 and %283 booked, and the law that answers it is four days old. A number read from a fetched spine is fresh for as long as nobody else is working, which on an eight-ship pier is no time at all -- so fetch-before-book buys a smaller window rather than a guarantee, and the stamp is what actually holds.
obs I killed my own stale pass by the PID I was handed and pkill -P against that PID alone, never by name. Three peer passes were live on the pier at that moment and none was touched.
